---
{%- set cp = countyProfiles %}
{%- set floodOnly = cp.floodOnly %}

<section class="cp-topic" id="coverage">
<h2>Coverage</h2>
<p class="cp-note">Each county has four topics. A topic is either available or unavailable for a stated reason. The reasons are rules, not shrugs; they are listed under the table.</p>
<div class="cp-table-wrap">
<table class="cp-matrix">
<thead>
<tr><th scope="col">County</th>{% for t in cp.topics %}<th scope="col">{{ t.label }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{%- for c in cp.counties %}
<tr>
<th scope="row"><a href="{{ ('/county-profiles/county/' ~ c.slug ~ '/') | url }}">{{ c.name }}</a><span class="cp-matrix-tier">{{ c.tierLabel }}</span></th>
{%- for t in c.coverage %}
{%- if t.available %}
<td class="is-available"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> Available</td>
{%- else %}
<td class="is-unavailable" title="{{ cp.reasonsById[t.reason].rule }}"><span aria-hidden="true">○</span> {{ t.reasonLabel }}</td>
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
</tr>
{%- endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Reasons a topic is unavailable</h3>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
{%- for r in cp.reasons %}
<div><dt id="reason-{{ r.id }}">{{ r.label }} <code>{{ r.id }}</code></dt><dd>{{ r.rule }}</dd></div>
{%- endfor %}
</dl>
<p class="cp-note"><strong>Zero is not unavailable.</strong> A count of 0, such as no medical facilities in a county's floodplain, is an answer and is shown as one. Unavailable means there is no data, and the reason is given. A figure the publisher withholds is a third state, shown as "withheld". <a href="{{ '/county-profiles/about/methodology/' | url }}">How the figures are computed</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="why-these-counties">
<h2>Why these {{ cp.counties | length }} counties</h2>
<p>The list is inherited from NOAA's own Coastal County Snapshots rather than defined afresh. {{ cp.enowCount }} of the counties are NOAA's Economics: National Ocean Watch (ENOW) list for California: a modified coastal-county definition that drops shore-adjacent counties with no relevant ocean economy and adds inland counties that have one. The rest are kept because working flood data exists for them.</p>
<p>The atlas therefore covers counties reachable by tide and by Delta flooding, not shoreline counties alone. That is a more accurate picture of California's exposure than a shoreline-only definition, and it follows from CASCA being a coastal resilience atlas rather than a sea-level-rise-only project.</p>
<h3>Coverage tiers</h3>
<dl class="cp-tiers">
{%- for t in cp.tiers %}
<div>
<dt>{{ t.label }} <span class="cp-tier-count">{{ t.counties | length }} counties</span></dt>
<dd>{{ t.summary }} {{ t.names | join(", ") }}.</dd>
</div>
{%- endfor %}
</dl>
<h3>Why {{ floodOnly.names | join(", ") }} are included</h3>
<p>These {{ floodOnly.counties | length }} counties are not coastal under any NOAA definition. They appear in NOAA's original because FEMA digital flood data existed for them when the snapshots were built: availability, not geography, put them on the list. They are kept because their flood figures are real and usable, and dropping them would discard working data over a naming argument. Each one states its coverage at the top of its profile, so a reader does not have to infer it from missing sections.</p>
</section>


<section class="cp-topic" id="economy-years">
<h2>Economy data years</h2>
{%- set em = countyProfiles.economyMethod %}
{%- if em %}
<p>Each source shows its own newest year, and the pages do not blend them silently. The headline year for jobs, wages and establishments is the Bureau of Labor Statistics' Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages (QCEW), <strong>{{ em.qcew }}</strong>. GDP comes from the Bureau of Economic Analysis one year behind for the marine sectors, <strong>{{ em.beaMarine }}</strong>, because BEA publishes the detailed industries a year after the broad sectors (total economy GDP is {{ em.beaTotal }}). The wages comparison slides use the newest year the comparison series covers, so the county's dot and the comparison dots are the same year: <strong>{{ em.qcewWages }}</strong> for the marine economy (NOAA Open ENOW, {{ em.openEnow }}) and <strong>{{ em.totalWages }}</strong> for the total economy (NOAA Total Economy (Coastal), {{ em.coastalEconomy }}). The shoreline share of tourism and recreation uses Census ZIP Code Business Patterns {{ em.zbp }}. <em>Total Jobs</em> shows employed workers and self-employed workers as two separate figures, each with its own year and source, and does not add them: for the total economy, QCEW {{ em.qcew }} and Census Nonemployer Statistics {{ em.nes }}; for the marine economy, QCEW {{ em.qcew }} and NOAA's ENOW self-employed series, which ends in {{ em.enowSelf }}.</p>
{%- endif %}
<p>The delta counties (Sacramento, San Joaquin and Yolo) are on ENOW's list but are not in the shore-adjacent total economy series, so they have a marine economy and no total economy. Their sea level rise comes from NOAA's Delta inundation file. Lake, San Benito, Sutter and Trinity are in neither ENOW nor NOAA's sea level rise data, and have flood hazard only. Which counties have which topics did not change when the economy figures were rebuilt.</p>
</section>
